A||N||24||kornholio||temp gauge goes up in traffic jams||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||11-05-1999||07:12 PM||||My temp gauge goes from 165 degrees F to 220 degrees F only in slow traffic.  Once I'm out of the heavy stop-and-go traffic, the temp gauge drops back to 165.  What is the cause of this?  I've seen other cars with this problem.  Does my thermostat need replacing?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||11-05-1999||07:20 PM||||I wouldn't worry about it. Most Fieros do that. Your fan switches on at around 230F. I've seen mine go as high as 260F (granted, that was when I had a leak in the radiator) and that didn't cause any problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-05-1999||08:14 PM||||It's a normal thing.  When you drive faster there is more air flowing through the radiator allowing for more cooling of the coolant.  As you slow down, less air is being passed through the raidator, allowing for higher temps.  I would make sure the fan does work though, just in case.  Thermostat shouldn't really affect this.  If it were bad, it would close, overheating the engine no matter how fast you go.  I'd say it's normal too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-05-1999||08:20 PM||||it's normal. <P>It's why some of us have put in an extra switch for the fan so we can lock it on in heavy traffic. If you live where there is allot of heavy traffic or it gets hot often then the switch is cheap help.<P>Other people put in fan switches that turn on at a lower temp. same end result but automatic operation.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||11-05-1999||08:26 PM||||I've noticed that when the temp goes up, I can lower the reading on the gauge by turning on the heat.  Does turning on the heat activate the fan?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||11-05-1999||08:42 PM||||The heater doesn't turn on the rad fan but when you turn on the heater you add another rad to the system with its own fan hence more cooling||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-06-1999||12:44 PM||||actually turning on the heat will in most years bring down engine temp..... it also activates the AC which activates the main fan.<P>even tho you are heating the logic is to use the AC to dehumidify the air. It makes the car dryer and the defog work better. In a small car like fiero dehumidification is important. You bring in allot of water that has to be removed every time you open the door.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-06-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||11-06-1999||11:40 PM||||For the temp to reach 220F in traffic is normal, most cars will do the same thing, but for it to dip down to 165F is not within normal operating temperature. If your engine is running at around 165 fahrenheit, than this could mean that your thermostat is not working prperly. It should be no lower than 190-195F. An engine that dose not operate at normal operating temperature and operates below it will build up with sludge and shorten the engines life. You will also get worse gas mileage and higher emissions. changing the thermostat only costs about five dollars anyway, its easy in a fiero.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||11-07-1999||05:13 AM||||Fiero56, this is nitpicking, but a thermostat can go bad by sticking open too. This happened on my Suburban one winter. The engine took forever to warm up, which meant I was very cold because there was no heat. A themostat sticking closed is worse, because like you said, it can overheat the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||11-07-1999||08:39 AM||||I don't mind your "nitpicking", I don't think I'd ever heard of a faulty thermostat sticking open.  I don't know if you guys have ever heard of the "failsafe" thermostats.  They are designed a little differently so that if/when they go bad, they go bad in the "open" position, preventing a roasted engine.  I just figured "normal" ones failed closed.  Well, you learn something new every day.<P>Hey, looks like I'm the first person to use a smiley on this thread. It's all from my happy nature <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-07-1999).]||||reg||7
Z||000010||kornholio||11-07-1999||12:24 PM||||My car has always ran at 165 degrees F.  This means that my thermostat needs replacing?  So, I should replace my thermostat to have my engine run hotter?  Do I need to burp the cooling system if I pull out the thermostat to add some coolant?||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||11-07-1999||04:16 PM||||reading 165 could be the thermostat bad, could even be a 165 thermostat.  could be the gauge is off. The thermostat temp is printed on the stat. usually on the bulb.<P>If you have a 165 stat you should make sure of what chip is in the ECM. Generally you can't run the stock chip with those low temp stats and get the best operation.<P>to check what's going on you need an ECM scanner, or a pyrometer. <P>The ECM scanner will tell you 2 things you want to know. 1. what is the coolant temp in degrees F reported to the ECM. 2. Is the ECM in closed loop mode. The coolant temp reported to the ECM is usually more acurate than the dash gauge, and reads in 1 degree steps. If the car spends allot of time in open loop you are wasting gas and poluting the air. While you may gain a bit of performance under certain conditions, the car is actually performing worse over all in open loop.<P>The pyrometer can tell you what the coolant temp is. Just stick it on the thermostat neck with a little grease and wait for the temp to settle. This lets you monitor coolant temp to see if the thermostat is opening when it should without opening the system.<P>If you are really running a 165, and have the factory ECM chip, then replace it with a 185 thermostat. The ECM will work correctly with that using the stock chip. You'll still gain some of the benifits of the 165, but for about 9$us compaired to 2-300$us for the aftermarket ECM chips required to run 165 properly.||||reg||1
Z||000012||kornholio||11-07-1999||05:51 PM||||How do I know what kind of ECM I have?  Does it say right on the chip or do I need to test it?<P>I tried to pull off the theremostat cap off, but it's stuck on and it's getting dark, so I'll try tomorrow.<P>Do I need to burp the cooling system if I just remove the thermostat cap after checking the thermostat?<P>Thanks for all the help!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Bill Osmer||11-07-1999||06:39 PM||||theOgre is right (again) - the ECM and it's temp sensor is the more accurate of the two possibles. The temp gauge is not really ever meant to read out with high accuracy (if you look at recent temp gauges, the OEs evn take most of the numbers off!). The gauge is meant to give a RELATIVE indication of coolant temperature, and will read differently from one Fiero to the next. Getting a correlation reading from the ECM to know where 165 (or ?) degress is on your gauge is a good thing to do. You can also substitute a 55 ohm resistor in the sensor circuit and see where your gauge reads (it should be 260 degrees, but will different on all cars)||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||11-08-1999||11:15 AM||||The cap for my thermostat was stuck when I bought my car.  So I just grabbed a big old pipe wrench and it took the cap off. Now that it's not frozen on there any more, I can just take it on and off by hand.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||11-08-1999||01:07 PM||||What motor was this?<P>the ECM prom can be identified with an ECM scanner, at least the OEM ones can. You may need to pull the prom cover off the ECM. The OEM ones are labled, the after market ones should be.<P>If the cap is rusted on, get another before you take it off. you could damage the old one, or the gasket may be to old to reseal well. They are a standard Stant item. they are less than 10$.||||reg||1
Z||000016||William Federle||11-08-1999||02:14 PM||||I just put a new thermostat in my 1984<BR>fiero (the old one had two cuts in the<BR>o-ring). It warms up faster but when it<BR>gets above the 1st mark on the gage (about<BR>160 deg) the check engine lite sometimes<BR>comes on and the code turns out to be a 13<BR>(oxygen sensor). I can easily bring the<BR>temp back down with my manual fan sw. but<BR>do I have a bad O2 sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||11-08-1999||06:25 PM||||Actually, 165F is just about the temperature you get when there's no thermostat <B>at all</B>... A while back when I had my radiator replaced, they didn't replace my thermostat (and didn't tell me) and temperature was always around 165.<P>BTW, they <B>still</B> try to convince me that running without a thermostat is better than with... Yeah right, I had always suspected there was this huge conspiracy by car manufacturers to put stuff in our cars that really makes the car run worse.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||William Federle||11-08-1999||07:31 PM||||I've suspected that my temperature gage may<BR>not be accurate (or even close). I would<BR>think that it would continue to rise to <BR>about 200 deg and then go down but it just<BR>hovers around the 160 mark. I think that the<BR>real temperature may be 200. I've got to <BR>find a way to check the gage.||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||11-09-1999||02:21 AM||||Will,,,<P>there are a few easy, all be it a bit costly, way to verify the gauge & sender as a set.<P>an IR or contact pyrometer. read temp of the thermostat neck. Decent Pyrometers are from 100-200$us.<P>An ECM scanner. The ECM will report the temp its reading to the scanner. The AutoXray GM only scanner is about 150$us.<P>an imersion thermometer. Cheap but dangerous if not used right. you can get an imersion unit for 15-150$us. Problem is getting it into the coolant, preferably with the system hot and pressurized. That means clamping it under the hose at the thermostat neck or tapping the water jacket neither of which is easy or mess free.||||reg||1
Z||000020||kornholio||11-09-1999||08:38 PM||||I checked my thermostat and it was a 195F.  I thought it might be faulty, so I bought a new one rated at 195F.  Install it and I still get 165F on the temp gauge when engine is warm.  I guess my temp gauge or sensor is messed.  Assuming that the temp gauge and sensor is working fine, is there anything else that could cause my 165F reading?<P>Here's a tip to remove stubborn thermostat caps:  Put on a pair of tight fitting rubber dish washing gloves.  The extra grip made taking the cap off much easier.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Patrick||11-09-1999||08:55 PM||||<BR><B>Rubber dish washing gloves</B> ??!!!<P>Have you no shame?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000022||fiero56||11-09-1999||08:59 PM||||I hear Martha Stewart calling for more tips already.||||reg||12
Z||000023||kornholio||11-09-1999||09:11 PM||||I wish I would have thought of the gloves before I tried removing it with my bare hands.  I scraped the $hit out of them!  Those tabs are sharp!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Patrick||11-09-1999||09:14 PM||||<BR>Us old-timers use shop rags.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
